WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
What I have here is an account&nbsp;
of an exam taken by Terence Tao,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.160
one of the world's best mathematicians,&nbsp;
while he was in graduate school.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:15.180
What's interesting about this exam is that Terence&nbsp;
Tao nearly failed. This Close call could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:21.840
thwarted his math career. But instead, he says&nbsp;
that this near failure is what propelled him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:28.800
to succeed. It was the results of this exam that&nbsp;
made Terence Tao stop playing so many video games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.200
and knuckle down and try harder. It was&nbsp;
the Princeton general qualifying exams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:41.220
which Terence took at age 18 after a year&nbsp;
of graduate work. At the time, around 1993,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:47.820
the Princeton graduate classes didn't have any&nbsp;
homework or tests. And this exam was the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:53.940
major thing that you needed to pass in order&nbsp;
to proceed with your PhD. It was an oral exam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.380
kind of like an interview where three&nbsp;
professors directly asked questions.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:03.720
Terence Tao is one of today's greatest&nbsp;
mathematicians and has won many awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.820
including a Fields Medal. On this channel&nbsp;
before I have covered an exam that he aced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.220
as a child prodigy, so I was a little surprised&nbsp;
to hear that he had ever struggled with an exam.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.140
We know what questions Terence was asked,&nbsp;
thanks to a tradition at Princeton of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.780
students writing up their memory of the exam&nbsp;
afterwards. This serves as a useful tool for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.940
other students wanting to know what to expect.&nbsp;
This is what Terence wrote after his exam.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:35.040
My examiners were Stein, Klainerman and&nbsp;
Rudnick. Klainerman came in about five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.780
minutes late. The first questions they&nbsp;
asked were about harmonic analysis. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.940
asked me what I knew. And I said, basically&nbsp;
singular integrals and functional analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:50.400
no real harmonic theory at all. So they&nbsp;
examined me on singular integrals instead.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.140
For every math student, the general exam&nbsp;
will draw questions from five topics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:01.800
real analysis, complex analysis, algebra,&nbsp;
and then two topics of the students choice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.640
Probably what they want to pursue their PhD in.&nbsp;
Terence's special topics were harmonic analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.340
and analytic number theory. Give examples&nbsp;
of singular integrals (Calderon-Zygmund),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:20.460
what theorems do you know about them? And how do&nbsp;
you prove boundedness? I stated a T(b) theorem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:25.560
which nobody seemed very acquainted with,&nbsp;
and said some brief explanation that knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:31.080
boundedness on L2 was sufficient to prove&nbsp;
it for LP for P between one and infinity.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:37.320
Harmonic analysis is the representation of&nbsp;
functions as waves, and includes Fourier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.960
transforms. The questions that Terence was&nbsp;
asked right off the bat are advanced. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.920
don't understand the answers, but that's&nbsp;
because they're on very specific topics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:53.100
You could make it all the way through a math&nbsp;
undergrad and PhD without ever encountering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.500
Calderon-Zygmund results of singular&nbsp;
integrals. These topics are what Terence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.060
has told the examiners he is prepared for, and&nbsp;
so that's where they're guiding the questions.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:10.260
Since my T(b) theorem was martingale based, Stein&nbsp;
asked me to give a definition of martingales. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.600
gave the sigma algebra purely aeasure theoretic&nbsp;
version, but stumbled a bit on the definition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.240
of conditional expectation. I think Stein&nbsp;
was satisfied by my explanation at the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.220
though. Stein then asked me what other&nbsp;
boundedness theorems I knew, I couldn't think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:32.340
any concrete ones offhand. But I mumbled about a&nbsp;
convolution operator being bounded if its Fourier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:38.040
transform was bounded, and had some smoothness&nbsp;
condition. But I couldn't recall any details.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.760
It's here that we see things&nbsp;
starting to unravel for Terence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.740
and each step going forwards, he's being&nbsp;
asked to demonstrate a complex idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:53.580
but chokes up on the details and in providing&nbsp;
concrete examples. Terence had studied harmonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:59.100
analysis for his master's thesis, and later&nbsp;
said that since he felt it was his strong suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:06.300
he only spent a few days reviewing analysis. This&nbsp;
T(b) theorem was part of his master's thesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:11.460
so he does okay with questions about it.&nbsp;
But as soon as they move away from it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:16.860
his lack of preparation is starting to show.&nbsp;
There were some questions about the boundedness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:23.220
of the Hilbert operator and an easy way of proof.&nbsp;
I said the Fourier transform was bounded, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:29.190
should do it for L2. They seem to be hinting&nbsp;
at generalisations and eventually I clicked on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:34.740
to Riesz potentials, but again, I didn't know&nbsp;
enough results, only that they were bounded.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:40.980
With this being an oral exam. We see how different&nbsp;
it is to a printed one. There are no set questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:47.280
and the examiners are coming up with questions&nbsp;
that respond to Terence's answers. This allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.500
them to really find the depth of his knowledge&nbsp;
in topics that he's supposed to be prepared for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.800
The format can be pretty intimidating. At age&nbsp;
18, Terence would have been a few years younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:05.100
than his fellow students. and had found math easy&nbsp;
ever since he was a young kid. With his natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:11.580
interest and talent for the subject, he had never&nbsp;
had to try too hard studying for an exam in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:17.940
past. And in earlier studies could get by with&nbsp;
cramming the textbook a few days before the exam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:23.880
and spending the rest of his time just playing&nbsp;
with the ideas that he found most interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:29.220
According to an article he wrote years&nbsp;
later, looking back at this exam, he said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:34.620
that he had made it all the way to Princeton,&nbsp;
without developing systematic study habits.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:40.440
I was asked for a couple of definitions of H1, I&nbsp;
got the atomic definition, right, and the Riesz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:46.020
potentials, I've got the limit of harmonic&nbsp;
functions definition wrong, and I had no idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:52.560
how to prove that any of them were equivalent. So&nbsp;
they abandoned this after a while, but not until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.860
they made me realise that the latter definition&nbsp;
I had given was actually that of L1, not H1.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:04.740
Instead of treating the qualifying exam as&nbsp;
a top priority. Terence said that he spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:10.080
an embarrassingly large fraction of his time&nbsp;
as a graduate student messing around online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:16.200
which might be the most relatable thing I've&nbsp;
read about him yet. He discovered the World Wide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:21.840
Web in his first year, and played computer&nbsp;
games until late at night in the Graduate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.940
Student Dormitories computer room. While his&nbsp;
fellow classmates did months of preparation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:32.820
Terence said that he only did about two weeks&nbsp;
worth, he might have been used to getting away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:38.640
with doing less work than those around him.&nbsp;
But natural talent can only get you so far.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.620
Then they asked me about fundamental&nbsp;
solutions, I sloughed the exponents in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:49.140
the denominator a few times. So they began&nbsp;
asking me about homogeneity. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:54.660
with a lot of prodding, I got the exponents of&nbsp;
the Newtonian and Cauchy potentials correctly.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:07:00.960
Here, the examiners are asking easier and easier&nbsp;
questions to try and get him to a point where he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:07.260
able to give a satisfactory answer. And they're&nbsp;
walking him through a derivation of finding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:13.080
fundamental solutions of the Laplacian.&nbsp;
Terence later described this as painful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:19.140
To be clear, for me, none of this is easy,&nbsp;
and barely any of the questions are even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:25.380
comprehensible. But I suppose it's considered easy&nbsp;
for someone who has taken the right coursework.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:31.860
Klainerman asked me about the connections between&nbsp;
PDEs and singular integrals, but I didn't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:37.920
any more than vague generalities. Terence later&nbsp;
said that he had enjoyed playing with harmonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:44.160
analysis for its own sake, but had never paid&nbsp;
much attention to how it was used in other fields,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:51.120
such as PDEs. This was the all is lost moment.&nbsp;
But Terence's luck was about to change.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.120
Then they switch to analytic number theory.&nbsp;
Surprisingly, I fared much better on this topic.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:02.580
In a stroke of luck, only one of the three&nbsp;
examiners had an extensive background in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:09.420
number theory, but had mistakenly thought that&nbsp;
Terence's second special topic was algebraic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:15.120
number theory instead of analytic number&nbsp;
theory. And after realising his mistake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.940
he wasn't able to use any of the difficult&nbsp;
algebraic number theory questions which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:26.400
he had prepared, because that wasn't what&nbsp;
Terence needed to be tested on. So instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.480
Terence was given just the standard&nbsp;
analytic number theory questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:36.900
which weren't that difficult. Rudnick mainly&nbsp;
asked give several forms of the zeta function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:42.960
give one that allows analytic continuation,&nbsp;
prove the modularity of the theta function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:48.780
and thus derive the functional equation.&nbsp;
What do you know about the poles and zeros?

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:56.220
Analytic number theory is using the methods of&nbsp;
analysis to study number theory. The Riemann zeta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:03.120
function is perhaps the most famous example, where&nbsp;
complex analysis is used to study the distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:08.880
of prime numbers. State a form of the prime&nbsp;
number theorem. Throughout I didn't have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.440
back up anything with messy calculations,&nbsp;
Rudnick always stopped me when I tried.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:20.220
Talk about primes in arithmetic progressions,&nbsp;
give have an elementary proof of the infinitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:26.340
of primes of the form of four n minus three.&nbsp;
These were more standard questions which Terence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:32.520
had prepared for and so breezed through them. The&nbsp;
prime number one is funny because Terence later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:38.760
went on to prove quite a famous theorem about&nbsp;
prime number progressions, which are a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:45.960
of numbers which are equally spaced. For example,&nbsp;
three, seven and 11 constitute a progression of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:53.220
prime numbers, where the gap between each number&nbsp;
is four. Terence Tao and Ben Green proved that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:58.800
it is always possible to find somewhere in the&nbsp;
infinity of integers, a progression of prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:05.700
numbers of equal spacing and any length. What&nbsp;
algebraic tools are used in Dirichlet's proof of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:11.820
infinitude of primes in arithmetic progressions?&nbsp;
It took a while before I understood, Oh, you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:17.640
me to talk about characters? And they asked how&nbsp;
Dirichlet got an explicit formula for this when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:24.540
Chi was a real character. I was going to write&nbsp;
a messy but finite expression involving sines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:30.660
and logs, but then realised that they were talking&nbsp;
about the class number formula. I said carelessly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:35.820
though, that this was a disgusting way to do&nbsp;
it. Since I was still thinking about the sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:41.100
log formulas. Then they made a comment that&nbsp;
this would put 1000s of people out of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:47.460
or something like that. Anyway, I stated what&nbsp;
the class number was vaguely, but didn't give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:53.400
out an explicit equation. Again, Rudnick waved me&nbsp;
off when I tried this, and they seem satisfied.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:58.680
It seems like here that Terence has&nbsp;
accidentally insulted anybody who uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:03.720
the class number theorem. But when he said&nbsp;
it was disgusting, it was because he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:09.720
thinking about writing out some other messy&nbsp;
expression. A nice, awkward miscommunication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:15.840
but maybe they all laughed about it. Then Rudnick&nbsp;
asked a few algebra questions only and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:22.380
over. Talk about Galois theory. What do you know&nbsp;
about A_n? What other simple groups do you know?

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:28.740
The monster but I backed down quickly, saying I&nbsp;
knew not how to define it. Then the discussion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:33.180
meandered a bit as the professor's talked&nbsp;
among themselves about whether the group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:40.440
of order 168 which I mentioned was sporadic.&nbsp;
After this, they decided to barely pass me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:46.560
though they said that my harmonic analysis&nbsp;
was far from satisfactory. They didn't ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:52.260
any real or complex analysis, but I guess from&nbsp;
my handling of the special topics, they decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:58.620
that wasn't necessary. Besides, we were almost&nbsp;
getting snowed in. The exam lasted two hours.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:05.100
After the exam Stein, who was his thesis&nbsp;
advisor, told Terence that he was disappointed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:10.800
that his letters of recommendation had been&nbsp;
strong, and this wasn't what he was expecting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:18.060
Terence said that this moment left him in a state&nbsp;
of shock because it was the first time he had done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:24.420
poorly on an exam that he had been genuinely&nbsp;
interested in doing well in. It's not the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:30.900
time he had done poorly on an exam. In fact, he&nbsp;
had failed two of them back in undergrad. But one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:37.860
was for a Fortran class where he had refused to&nbsp;
learn it on the basis that he already knew how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:44.940
program in BASIC. And the other one was a quantum&nbsp;
mechanics class, where Terence ignored the warning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:50.220
that he would have to write an essay about the&nbsp;
history of the subject during the final exam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:56.160
and he had to be escorted from that final exam&nbsp;
in tears. He said that the Princeton general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:03.540
exam was a wake up call and a turning point&nbsp;
in his career, he began to take studying and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:09.480
his classes more seriously, because he had&nbsp;
finally seen consequences for not studying.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.900
I listened more to my fellow&nbsp;
students and other faculty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:18.300
and I cut back on my gaming, I worked&nbsp;
particularly hard on all the problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:23.760
that my advisor gave me in the hopes of&nbsp;
finally impressing him. In retrospect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:29.940
nearly failing the generals was probably the best&nbsp;
thing that could have happened to me at the time.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:35.820
Being told that his harmonic analysis&nbsp;
was unsatisfactory is kind of funny now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:40.620
because Terence Tao was awarded the&nbsp;
Fields Medal for his contributions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:46.380
to partial differential equation,s&nbsp;
combinatorics, harmonic analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:52.200
and additive number theory. Either he did&nbsp;
become a lot better at harmonic analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:58.740
or these exams are not great at and measuring&nbsp;
one's ability to get a Fields Medal. Reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:04.980
about Terence Tao struggles with this exam is i'm&nbsp;
sure comforting to anyone who has struggled with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:11.940
test. It's a reminder that tests themselves don't&nbsp;
say too much about your potential. But instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:18.600
what's more important is what you do after getting&nbsp;
a bad result. It also shows that really dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:24.960
and intelligent people can sometimes mess up&nbsp;
opportunities by overestimating their own ability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:32.160
or assuming they can coast by purely on natural&nbsp;
talent. Terence Tao is a top tier mathematician&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:38.760
because of his creativity and problem solving and&nbsp;
in coming up with new mathematics. These aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:45.300
skills that are being tested on a qualifying&nbsp;
exam, but experience and being well read also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:51.900
matter. Terence himself says that one does not&nbsp;
need a genius gene to become a mathematician.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:58.200
Talent is important of course, but how one&nbsp;
develops and nurtures it is even more so.

